<DIV class=3D"hatnote">"Fortran language features" redirects here. For =
features of=20
previous versions of the language, see <A title=3D"Fortran" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#History">Fortran=20
=C2=A7 History</A>.</DIV>
<P>This is an overview of <B>Fortran 95 language features</B>. Included =
are the=20
additional features of TR-15581:Enhanced Data Type Facilities, that have =
been=20
universally implemented. Old features that have been superseded by new =
ones are=20
not described =E2=80=94 few of those historic features are used in =
modern programs=20
although most have been retained in the language to maintain <A =
title=3D"Backward compatibility"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backward_compatibility">backward=20
compatibility</A>. Although the current standard is Fortran 2008, even =
many of=20
those features first introduced into Fortran 2003 are still being=20
implemented.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-1"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#cite_n=
ote-1"><SPAN>[</SPAN>1<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP>=20
The additional features of Fortran 2003 and Fortran 2008 are described =
by=20
Metcalf, Reid and Cohen.<SUP class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-2"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#cite_n=
ote-2"><SPAN>[</SPAN>2<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP><SUP=20
class=3D"reference" id=3D"cite_ref-3"><A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#cite_n=
ote-3"><SPAN>[</SPAN>3<SPAN>]</SPAN></A></SUP></P>
<P></P>
