<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Language_elements">Language=20
elements</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Language elements"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<P>Fortran is <A title=3D"Case-insensitive" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Case-insensitive">case-insensitive<=
/A>.=20
The convention of writing Fortran keywords in upper case and all other =
names in=20
lower case is adopted in this article; except, by way of contrast, in =
the=20
input/output descriptions (<A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#Data_t=
ransfer">Data=20
transfer</A> and <A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#Operat=
ions_on_external_files">Operations=20
on external files</A>).</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Basics">Basics</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Basics" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>The basic component of the Fortran language is its <I>character =
set</I>. Its=20
members are</P>
<UL>
  <LI>the letters A ... Z and a ... z (which are equivalent outside a =
character=20
  context)</LI>
  <LI>the numerals 0 ... 9</LI>
  <LI>the underscore _</LI>
  <LI>the special characters <CODE>=3D &nbsp;: + blank - * / ( ) [ ] , . =
$=20
  '&nbsp;! " &nbsp;% &amp; &nbsp;; &lt; &gt; &nbsp;?</CODE></LI></UL>
<P><A title=3D"Token (parser)" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Token_(parser)">Tokens</A>=20
that have a syntactic meaning to the compiler are built from those =
components.=20
There are six classes of tokens:</P>
<TABLE>
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TH>Label</TH>
    <TD><CODE>123</CODE></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TH>Constant</TH>
    <TD><CODE>123.456789_long</CODE></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TH>Keyword</TH>
    <TD><CODE>ALLOCATABLE</CODE></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TH>Operator</TH>
    <TD><CODE>.add.</CODE></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TH>Name</TH>
    <TD><CODE>solve_equation</CODE> (up to 31 characters, including =
_)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TH>Separator</TH>
    <TD><CODE>/ ( ) (/ /) [ ] , =3D =3D&gt; &nbsp;: &nbsp;:: &nbsp;;=20
    &nbsp;%</CODE></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
<P>From the tokens, <A title=3D"Statement (programming)" =
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statement_(programming)">statements=
</A>=20
are built. These can be coded using the new free <I>source form</I> =
which does=20
not require positioning in a rigid column structure:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">FUNCTION </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">s2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>                          =
   <SPAN class=3D"c">! This is a comment</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">INTENT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">IN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s2</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">//</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">&amp;</SPAN>=0A=
      <SPAN class=3D"n">s2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>                      =
    <SPAN class=3D"c">! This is a continuation</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">+</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">s2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">END FUNCTION </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Note the trailing comments and the trailing continuation mark. There =
may be=20
39 continuation lines, and 132 characters per line. Blanks are =
significant.=20
Where a token or character constant is split across two lines:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE>               <SPAN class=3D"p">...</SPAN>        <SPAN =
class=3D"n">start_of</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">&amp;</SPAN>=0A=
        <SPAN class=3D"p">&amp;</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"err">_</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">name</SPAN>=0A=
               <SPAN class=3D"p">...</SPAN>   <SPAN class=3D"s1">'a very =
long &amp;</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"s1">        &amp;string'</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>a leading <CODE>&amp;</CODE> on the continued line is also =
required.</P>
<P>Automatic conversion of source form for existing programs can be =
carried out=20
by <A class=3D"external text" =
href=3D"ftp://ftp.numerical.rl.ac.uk/pub/MRandC/convert.f90"=20
rel=3D"nofollow">convert.f90</A>.</P>
<P>Its options are</P>
<UL>
  <LI>significant blank handling;</LI>
  <LI>indentation;</LI>
  <LI>CONTINUE replaced by END DO;</LI>
  <LI>name added to subprogram END statement; and</LI>
  <LI>INTEGER*2 etc. syntax converted.</LI></UL>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Intrinsic_data_types">Intrinsic =
data=20
types</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Intrinsic data types"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D3">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Fortran has five <I>intrinsic data types</I>: <CODE>INTEGER</CODE>,=20
<CODE>REAL</CODE>, <CODE>COMPLEX</CODE>, <CODE>LOGICAL</CODE> and=20
<CODE>CHARACTER</CODE>. Each of those types can be additionally =
characterized by=20
a <I>kind</I>. Kind, basically, defines internal representation of the =
type: for=20
the three numeric types, it defines the precision and range, and for the =
other=20
two, the specifics of storage representation. Thus, it is an abstract =
concept=20
which models the limits of data types' representation; it is expressed =
as a=20
member of a set of whole numbers (e.g. it may be {1, 2, 4, 8} for =
integers,=20
denoting bytes of storage), but those values are not specified by the =
Standard=20
and not portable. For every type, there is a <I>default kind</I>, which =
is used=20
if no kind is explicitly specified. For each intrinsic type, there is a=20
corresponding form of <I>literal constant</I>. The numeric types=20
<CODE>INTEGER</CODE> and <CODE>REAL</CODE> can only be signed (there is =
no=20
concept of sign for type <CODE>COMPLEX</CODE>).</P>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Literal_constants_and_kinds">Literal constants=20
and kinds</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Literal constants and kinds" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D4">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<H5><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"INTEGER">INTEGER</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
INTEGER" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D5">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H5>
<P>Integer literal constants of the default kind take the form</P>
<PRE>1   0   -999   32767   +10=0A=
</PRE>
<P>Kind can be defined as a named constant. If the desired range is=20
=C2=B110<SUP>kind</SUP>, the portable syntax for defining the =
appropriate kind,=20
<CODE>two_bytes</CODE> is</P>
<PRE>INTEGER, PARAMETER :: two_bytes =3D SELECTED_INT_KIND(4)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>that allows subsequent definition of constants of the form</P>
<PRE>-1234_two_bytes   +1_two_bytes=0A=
</PRE>
<P>Here, <CODE>two_bytes</CODE> is the kind type parameter; it can also =
be an=20
explicit default integer literal constant, like</P>
<PRE>-1234_2=0A=
</PRE>
<P>but such use is non-portable.</P>
<P>The KIND function supplies the value of a kind type parameter:</P>
<PRE>KIND(1)            KIND(1_two_bytes)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>and the <CODE>RANGE</CODE> function supplies the actual decimal range =
(so the=20
user must make the actual mapping to bytes):</P>
<PRE>RANGE(1_two_bytes)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>Also, in <A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#DATA_s=
tatement"><CODE>DATA</CODE>=20
(initialization) statements</A>, binary (B), octal (O) and hexadecimal =
(Z)=20
constants may be used (often informally referred to as "BOZ =
constants"):</P>
<PRE>B'01010101'   O'01234567'   Z'10fa'=0A=
</PRE>
<H5><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"REAL">REAL</SPAN><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: REAL" =

href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D6">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H5>
<P>There are at least two real kinds=E2=80=94the default, and one with =
greater precision=20
(this replaces <CODE>DOUBLE PRECISION</CODE>). =
<CODE>SELECTED_REAL_KIND</CODE>=20
functions returns the kind number for desired range and precision; for =
at least=20
9 decimal digits of precision and a range of 10<SUP>=E2=88=9299</SUP> to =

10<SUP>99</SUP>, it can be specified as:</P>
<PRE>INTEGER, PARAMETER :: long =3D SELECTED_REAL_KIND(9, 99)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>and literals subsequently specified as</P>
<PRE>1.7_long=0A=
</PRE>
<P>Also, there are the intrinsic functions</P>
<PRE>KIND(1.7_long)   PRECISION(1.7_long)   RANGE(1.7_long)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>that give in turn the kind type value, the actual precision (here at =
least=20
9), and the actual range (here at least 99).</P>
<H5><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"COMPLEX">COMPLEX</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
COMPLEX" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D7">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H5>
<P><CODE>COMPLEX</CODE> data type is built of two integer or real=20
components:</P>
<PRE>(1, 3.7_long)=0A=
</PRE>
<H5><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"LOGICAL">LOGICAL</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
LOGICAL" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D8">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H5>
<P>There are only two basic values of logical constants: =
<CODE>.TRUE.</CODE> and=20
<CODE>.FALSE.</CODE>. Here, there may also be different kinds. Logicals =
don't=20
have their own kind inquiry functions, but use the kinds specified for=20
<CODE>INTEGER</CODE>s; default kind of <CODE>LOGICAL</CODE> is the same =
as of=20
INTEGER.</P>
<PRE>.FALSE.   .true._one_byte=0A=
</PRE>
<P>and the <CODE>KIND</CODE> function operates as expected:</P>
<PRE>KIND(.TRUE.)=0A=
</PRE>
<H5><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"CHARACTER">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
CHARACTER" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D9">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H5>
<P>The forms of literal constants for <CODE>CHARACTER</CODE> data type =
are</P>
<PRE>'A string'   "Another"   'A "quote"'   ''=0A=
</PRE>
<P>(the last being an empty string). Different kinds are allowed (for =
example,=20
to distinguish <A title=3D"ASCII"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASCII">ASCII</A> and <A =
title=3D"UNICODE"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNICODE">UNICODE</A>=20
strings), but not widely supported by compilers. Again, the kind value =
is given=20
by the <CODE>KIND</CODE> function:</P>
<PRE> KIND('ASCII')=0A=
</PRE>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Number_model_and_intrinsic_functions">Number=20
model and intrinsic functions</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Number model and intrinsic functions" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D10">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P>The numeric types are based on number models with associated inquiry=20
functions (whose values are independent of the values of their =
arguments;=20
arguments are used only to provide kind). These functions are important =
for=20
portable numerical software:</P>
<TABLE class=3D"wikitable">
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>DIGITS(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Number of significant digits</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>EPSILON(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Almost negligible compared to one (real)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>HUGE(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Largest number</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>MAXEXPONENT(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Maximum model exponent (real)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>MINEXPONENT(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Minimum model exponent (real)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>PRECISION(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Decimal precision (real, complex)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>RADIX(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Base of the model</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>RANGE(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Decimal exponent range</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>TINY(X)</CODE></TD>
    <TD>Smallest positive number (real)</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Scalar_variables">Scalar =
variables</SPAN><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Scalar variables"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D11">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Scalar <A title=3D"Variable (programming)" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variable_(programming)">variables</=
A>=20
corresponding to the five intrinsic types are specified as follows:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">INTEGER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">KIND</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">i</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">KIND</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">long</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">COMPLEX</SPAN>         <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">current</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">LOGICAL</SPAN>         <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">Pravda</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">20</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">word</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"nb">KIND</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">Kanji</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">kanji_word</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>where the optional <CODE>KIND</CODE> parameter specifies a =
non-default kind,=20
and the <CODE>::</CODE> notation delimits the type and attributes from =
variable=20
name(s) and their optional initial values, allowing full variable =
specification=20
and initialization to be typed in one statement (in previous standards,=20
attributes and initializers had to be declared in several statements). =
While it=20
is not required in above examples (as there are no additional attributes =
and=20
initialization), most Fortran-90 programmers acquire the habit to use it =

everywhere.</P>
<P><CODE>LEN=3D</CODE> specifier is applicable only to =
<CODE>CHARACTER</CODE>s and=20
specifies the string length (replacing the older <CODE>*len</CODE> =
form). The=20
explicit <CODE>KIND=3D</CODE> and <CODE>LEN=3D</CODE> specifiers are =
optional:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">Kanji</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">kanji_word</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>works just as well.</P>
<P>There are some other interesting character features. Just as a =
substring as=20
in</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">line</SPAN>   =0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"p">...</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">line</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>                     <SPAN =
class=3D"c">! substring</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>was previously possible, so now is the substring</P>
<PRE>'0123456789'(i:i)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>Also, zero-length strings are allowed:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"n">line</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">-</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>       <SPAN =
class=3D"c">! zero-length string</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Finally, there is a set of intrinsic character functions, examples =
being</P>
<TABLE class=3D"wikitable">
  <TBODY>
  <TR>
    <TD style=3D"width: 50%;"><CODE>ACHAR</CODE></TD>
    <TD><CODE>IACHAR</CODE> (for ASCII set)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>ADJUSTL</CODE></TD>
    <TD><CODE>ADJUSTR</CODE></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>LEN_TRIM</CODE></TD>
    <TD><CODE>INDEX(s1, s2, BACK=3D.TRUE.)</CODE></TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>REPEAT</CODE></TD>
    <TD><CODE>SCAN</CODE>(for one of a set)</TD></TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><CODE>TRIM</CODE></TD>
    <TD><CODE>VERIFY</CODE>(for all of a set)</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Derived_data_types">Derived data=20
types</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Derived data types"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D12">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>For derived data types, the form of the type must be defined =
first:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">person</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">10</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">name</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL          </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">age</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">END TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">person</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>and then, variables of that type can be defined:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">person</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">you</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">me</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>To select components of a derived type, <CODE>%</CODE> qualifier is =
used:</P>
<PRE>you%age=0A=
</PRE>
<P>Literal constants of derived types have the form=20
<I><CODE>TypeName(1stComponentLiteral, 2ndComponentLiteral, =
...)</CODE></I>:</P>
<PRE>you =3D person('Smith', 23.5)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>which is known as a <I>structure constructor</I>. Definitions may =
refer to a=20
previously defined type:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">point</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">x</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">y</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">END TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">point</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">triangle</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">point</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">b</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">c</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">END TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">triangle</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>and for a variable of type triangle, as in</P>
<PRE>TYPE(triangle) t=0A=
</PRE>
<P>each component of type <CODE>point</CODE> is accessed as</P>
<PRE>t%a   t%b   t%c=0A=
</PRE>
<P>which, in turn, have ultimate components of type real:</P>
<PRE>t%a%x   t%a%y   t%b%x   etc.=0A=
</PRE>
<P>(Note that the <CODE>%</CODE> qualifier was chosen rather than dot=20
(<CODE>.</CODE>) because of potential ambiguity with operator notation, =
like=20
<CODE>.OR.</CODE>).</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Implicit_and_explicit_typing">Implicit and=20
explicit typing</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Implicit and explicit typing" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D13">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Unless specified otherwise, all variables starting with letters I, J, =
K, L, M=20
and N are default <CODE>INTEGER</CODE>s, and all others are default=20
<CODE>REAL</CODE>; other data types must be explicitly declared. This is =
known=20
as <I>implicit typing</I> and is a heritage of early FORTRAN days. Those =

defaults can be overridden by <I><CODE>IMPLICIT TypeName=20
(CharacterRange)</CODE></I> statements, like:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">IMPLICIT </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"kt">COMPLEX</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">Z</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">IMPLICIT </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">A</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">-</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">B</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">IMPLICIT </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">C</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">-</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">H</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">N</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">-</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">Y</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>However, it is a good practice to explicitly type all variables, and =
this can=20
be forced by inserting the statement</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">IMPLICIT NONE</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>at the beginning of each program unit.</P>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Arrays">Arrays</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Arrays" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D14">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Arrays are considered to be variables in their own right. Every array =
is=20
characterized by its <A title=3D"Type (computer programming)" =
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_(computer_programming)">type</=
A>, <A=20
title=3D"Rank (computer programming)" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rank_(computer_programming)">rank</=
A>,=20
and <I>shape</I> (which defines the extents of each dimension). Bounds =
of each=20
dimension are by default 1 and <I>size</I>, but arbitrary bounds can be=20
explicitly specified. <CODE>DIMENSION</CODE> keyword is optional and =
considered=20
an attribute; if omitted, the array shape must be specified after =
array-variable=20
name. For example,</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">10</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">INTEGER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">0</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">100</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">-</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">50</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">50</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">map</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>declares two arrays, rank-1 and rank-2, whose elements are in <A =
title=3D"Column-major order"=20
class=3D"mw-redirect"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Column-major_order">column-major =
order</A>.=20
Elements are, for example,</P>
<PRE>a(1)  a(i*j)=0A=
</PRE>
<P>and are scalars. The subscripts may be any scalar integer =
expression.</P>
<P><I>Sections</I> are parts of the array variables, and are arrays=20
themselves:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">j</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>               <SPAN =
class=3D"c">! rank one</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">map</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">j</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">k</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">l</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">m</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>      <SPAN class=3D"c">! =
rank two</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">map</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">k</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">l</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">))</SPAN>       <SPAN =
class=3D"c">! vector subscript</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>               <SPAN =
class=3D"c">! zero length</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Whole arrays and array sections are array-valued objects. =
Array-valued=20
constants (constructors) are available, enclosed in <CODE>(/ ... =
/)</CODE>:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">4</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mi">3</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">4</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">i</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">9</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">0</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">i</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mi">100</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mf">0.1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">*</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">i</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">10</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>making use of an implied-DO loop notation. Fortran 2003 allows the =
use of=20
brackets: <CODE>[1, 2, 3, 4]</CODE> and <CODE>[([1,2,3], =
i=3D1,4)]</CODE> instead=20
of the first two examples above, and many compilers support this now. A =
derived=20
data type may, of course, contain array components:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">vertex</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">END TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">4</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">t</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>so that</P>
<UL>
  <LI><CODE>t(2)</CODE> is a scalar (a structure)</LI>
  <LI><CODE>t(2)%vertex</CODE> is an array component of a =
scalar</LI></UL>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Data_initialization">Data=20
initialization</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Data initialization" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D15">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>Variables can be given initial values as specified in a specification =

statement:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">0.1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">0.2</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">0.3</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>and a default initial value can be given to the component of a =
derived data=20
type:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">vertex</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">0.0</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">END TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>When local variables are initialized within a procedure they =
implicitly=20
acquire the SAVE attribute:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">point</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">0.0</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">1.0</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">-</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mf">1.0</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>This declaration is equivalent to</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">SAVE</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">point</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">0.0</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">1.0</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">-</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mf">1.0</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>for local variables within a subroutine or function. The SAVE =
attribute=20
causes local variables to retain their value after a procedure call and =
then to=20
initialize the variable to the saved value upon returning to the =
procedure.</P>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"PARAMETER_attribute">PARAMETER=20
attribute</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: PARAMETER attribute" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D16">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P>A named constant can be specified directly by adding the=20
<CODE>PARAMETER</CODE> attribute and the constant values to a type=20
statement:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">PARAMETER</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">field</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">0.</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">1.</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">2.</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">PARAMETER</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">t</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">0.</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">0.</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mf">0.</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"DATA_statement">DATA =
statement</SPAN><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: DATA =
statement"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D17">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P>The <CODE>DATA</CODE> statement can be used for scalars and also for =
arrays=20
and variables of derived type. It is also the only way to initialise =
just parts=20
of such objects, as well as to initialise to binary, octal or =
hexadecimal=20
values:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">t1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">t2</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">DATA </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">t1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">triplet</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">0.</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">1.</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mf">2.</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">t2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">vertex</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">12</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mf">3.</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">DATA array</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">64</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mi">64</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">*</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">0</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">DATA </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">i</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">j</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">k</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">B</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"s1">'01010101'</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">O</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"s1">'77'</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">Z</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"s1">'ff'</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<H4><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Initialization_expressions">Initialization=20
expressions</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Initialization expressions" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D18">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H4>
<P>The values used in <CODE>DATA</CODE> and <CODE>PARAMETER</CODE> =
statements,=20
or with these attributes, are constant expressions that may include =
references=20
to: array and structure constructors, elemental intrinsic functions with =
integer=20
or character arguments and results, and the six transformational =
functions=20
<CODE>REPEAT, SELECTED_INT_KIND, TRIM, SELECTED_REAL_KIND, =
RESHAPE</CODE> and=20
<CODE>TRANSFER</CODE> (see <A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#Intrin=
sic_procedures">Intrinsic=20
procedures</A>):</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"kt">INTEGER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">PARAMETER</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"nb">long</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"nb">SELECTED_REAL_KIND</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">12</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN>   <SPAN =
class=3D"p">&amp;</SPAN>=0A=
                       <SPAN class=3D"k">array</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">/</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Specification_expressions">Specification=20
expressions</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Specification expressions" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D19">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>It is possible to specify details of variables using any =
non-constant,=20
scalar, integer expression that may also include inquiry function=20
references:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE><SPAN class=3D"k">SUBROUTINE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">s</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">b</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">m</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">USE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"nb">mod</SPAN>              =
                   <SPAN class=3D"c">! contains a</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(:,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">:)</SPAN>             <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">b</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">DIMENSION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">UBOUND</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">b</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">+</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"mi">5</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">x</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">INTEGER</SPAN>                           <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">m</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">=3D*</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>                  <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">m</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">+</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">))</SPAN>        <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">cc</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">REAL</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">SELECTED_REAL_KIND</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">*</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">PRECISION</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)))</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">z</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<H2><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Expressions_and_assignments">Expressions and=20
assignments</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Expressions and assignments" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D20">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H2>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Scalar_numeric">Scalar =
numeric</SPAN><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Scalar numeric"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D21">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>The usual arithmetic operators are available =E2=80=94 <CODE>+, -, *, =
/, **</CODE>=20
(given here in increasing order of precedence).</P>
<P>Parentheses are used to indicate the order of evaluation where =
necessary:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">*</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">b</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">+</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN>     <SPAN class=3D"c">! * first</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">*</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">b</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">+</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">c</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>   <SPAN class=3D"c">! + first</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>The rules for <I>scalar numeric</I> expressions and assignments =
accommodate=20
the non-default kinds. Thus, the mixed-mode numeric expression and =
assignment=20
rules incorporate different kind type parameters in an expected way:</P>
<PRE>real2 =3D integer0 + real1=0A=
</PRE>
<P>converts <CODE>integer0</CODE> to a real value of the same kind as=20
<CODE>real1</CODE>; the result is of same kind, and is converted to the =
kind of=20
<CODE>real2</CODE> for assignment.</P>
<P>These functions are available for controlled <A title=3D"Rounding" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rounding">rounding</A>=20
of real numbers to integers:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><CODE>NINT</CODE>: round to nearest integer, return integer =
result</LI>
  <LI><CODE>ANINT</CODE>: round to nearest integer, return real =
result</LI>
  <LI><CODE>INT</CODE>: truncate (round towards zero), return integer=20
result</LI>
  <LI><CODE>AINT</CODE>: truncate (round towards zero), return real =
result</LI>
  <LI><CODE>CEILING</CODE>: smallest integral value not less than =
argument=20
  (round up) (Fortran-90)</LI>
  <LI><CODE>FLOOR</CODE>: largest integral value not greater than =
argument=20
  (round down) (Fortran-90)</LI></UL>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" =
id=3D"Scalar_relational_operations">Scalar=20
relational operations</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Scalar relational operations" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D22">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>For <I>scalar relational</I> operations of numeric types, there is a =
set of=20
built-in operators:</P>
<PRE>&lt;    &lt;=3D    =3D=3D   /=3D   &gt;   &gt;=3D=0A=
.LT. .LE. .EQ. .NE. .GT. .GE.=0A=
</PRE>
<P>(the forms above are new to Fortran-90, and older equivalent forms =
are given=20
below them). Example expressions:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"n">a</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">&lt;</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">b</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">.</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"nb">AND</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">.</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">i</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">/=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">j</SPAN>      <SPAN class=3D"c">! for numeric =
variables</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">flag</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">a</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">b</SPAN>           <SPAN class=3D"c">! for logical variable =
flags</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Scalar_characters">Scalar=20
characters</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN =
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Scalar characters" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D23">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>In the case of <I>scalar characters</I> and given</P>
<PRE>CHARACTER(8) result=0A=
</PRE>
<P>it is legal to write</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">result</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">5</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"k">result</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>    <SPAN class=3D"c">! overlap allowed</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">result</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"k">result</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">3</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">2</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>    <SPAN class=3D"c">! no assignment of null =
string</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Concatenation is performed by the operator '//'.</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">result</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"s1">'abcde'</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">//</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"s1">'123'</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">filename</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">result</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">//</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"s1">'.dat'</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<H3><SPAN class=3D"mw-headline" id=3D"Derived-data_types">Derived-data=20
types</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mw-editsection"><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</SPAN><A title=3D"Edit section: =
Derived-data types"=20
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DFortran_95_language_=
features&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D24">edit</A><SPAN=20
class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</SPAN></SPAN></H3>
<P>No built-in operations (except assignment, defined on component-by =
component=20
basis) exist between <I>derived data types</I> mutually or with =
intrinsic types.=20
The meaning of existing or user-specified operators can be (re)defined=20
though:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">string80</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"kt">INTEGER       </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN>=0A=
   <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">value</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k"> END TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">string80</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN>    =
<SPAN class=3D"n">char1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">char2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">char3</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">str1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN>  <SPAN class=3D"n">str2</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN>  <SPAN class=3D"n">str3</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>we can write</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"n">str3</SPAN>  <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">str1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">//</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">str2</SPAN>       <SPAN class=3D"c">! must define =
operation</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">str3</SPAN>  <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">str1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">.</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">concat</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">.</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">str2</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"c">! must define operation</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">char3</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">char2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">//</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">char3</SPAN>     <SPAN class=3D"c">! intrinsic operator =
only</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">str3</SPAN>  <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">char1</SPAN>            <SPAN class=3D"c">! must define =
assignment</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>Notice the "<A title=3D"Operator overloading" =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operator_overloading">overloaded</A=
>"=20
use of the intrinsic symbol <CODE>//</CODE> and the named operator,=20
<CODE>.concat.</CODE> . A difference between the two cases is that, for =
an=20
intrinsic operator token, the usual precedence rules apply, whereas for =
named=20
operators, precedence is the highest as a unary operator or the lowest =
as a=20
binary one. In</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"n">vector3</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">matrix</SPAN>    <SPAN class=3D"o">*</SPAN>    <SPAN =
class=3D"n">vector1</SPAN>  <SPAN class=3D"o">+</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">vector2</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"n">vector3</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">matrix</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"p">.</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">times</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">.</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">vector1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">+</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">vector2</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>the two expressions are equivalent only if appropriate parentheses =
are added=20
as shown. In each case there must be defined, in a <A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#Module=
s">module</A>,=20
procedures defining the operator and assignment, and corresponding=20
operator-procedure association, as follows:</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">INTERFACE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">OPERATOR</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">//</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"c">!Overloads the // operator as invoking string_concat =
procedure</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">MODULE PROCEDURE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN>=0A=
 <SPAN class=3D"k">END INTERFACE</SPAN>=0A=
</PRE></DIV>
<P>The string concatenation function is a more elaborated version of =
that shown=20
already in <A =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran_95_language_features#Basics=
">Basics</A>.=20
Note that in order to handle the error condition that arises when the =
two=20
strings together exceed the preset 80-character limit, it would be safer =
to use=20
a subroutine to perform the concatenation (in this case =
operator-overloading=20
would not be applicable.)</P>
<DIV class=3D"mw-highlight mw-content-ltr" dir=3D"ltr">
<PRE> <SPAN class=3D"k">MODULE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_type</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">IMPLICIT NONE</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">    TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">string80</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"kt">INTEGER </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">length</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">80</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>   <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">END TYPE </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">string80</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">INTERFACE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">ASSIGNMENT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"k">MODULE PROCEDURE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">c_to_s_assign</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">s_to_c_assign</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">END INTERFACE</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">    INTERFACE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">OPERATOR</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">//</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"k">MODULE PROCEDURE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">END INTERFACE</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k"> CONTAINS</SPAN>=0A=
<SPAN class=3D"k">    SUBROUTINE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">c_to_s_assign</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">INTENT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">OUT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>    <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D*</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">INTENT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">IN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>  <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">c</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"n">s</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"n">s</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">END SUBROUTINE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">c_to_s_assign</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">SUBROUTINE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s_to_c_assign</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">c</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">s</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">INTENT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">IN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>     <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"nb">LEN</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"o">=3D*</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">INTENT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">OUT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">c</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"n">c</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">s</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"mi">1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">:</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">length</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">END SUBROUTINE </SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s_to_c_assign</SPAN>=0A=
    <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">FUNCTION </SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">string_concat</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s2</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">),</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"k">INTENT</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">IN</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s2</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"k">TYPE</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"n">s</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"kt">INTEGER</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> =
<SPAN class=3D"n">n1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">,</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">n2</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"kt">CHARACTER</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"mi">160</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"kd">::</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"n">ctot</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"n">n1</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN_TRIM</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"n">n2</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"nb">LEN_TRIM</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">s2</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">%</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">string_data</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN>=0A=
       <SPAN class=3D"k">IF</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"p">(</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">n1</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"o">+</SPAN><SPAN =
class=3D"n">n2</SPAN> <SPAN class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</SPAN> <SPAN =
class=3D"mi">80</SPAN><SPAN class=3D"p">)</SPAN> <SPAN =
